>
  <p>
    Franchisees get a proven concept, a brand book and a prototype design. What they don’t get is a building that is
    ready for that prototype. Every site has its own landlord, its own existing conditions and its own building
    department — and opening day is usually promised before any of that is fully understood. This checklist walks
    through what to line up, in order, so your build-out doesn’t become the reason your opening slips.
  </p>
  <p>
    Meridian has built franchise locations for <a href="/projects/aqua-tots-swim-schools/">Aqua-Tots Swim Schools</a>,{' '}
    <a href="/projects/nkd-pizza/">NKD Pizza</a> and <a href="/projects/menchies-frozen-yogurt/">Menchie’s Frozen Yogurt</a>{' '}
    across Michigan. The list below reflects how those projects come together.
  </p>

  <h2 id="before-lease">Before you sign the lease</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Confirm the space meets the franchisor’s site criteria — size, frontage, ceiling height, parking, signage visibility.</li>
    <li>Have a contractor walk the space. Existing utilities, HVAC capacity, plumbing locations and the condition of the slab can change the budget dramatically.</li>
    <li>For food concepts, check grease interceptor, venting and gas service requirements early — these are expensive to add.</li>
    <li>Negotiate the TI allowance and the landlord work letter with real numbers in hand, not after.</li>
    <li>Make sure the rent-commencement date leaves room for design, permits and construction.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="design">Prototype and site adaptation</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Get the franchisor’s current prototype drawings, brand standards and approved vendor list.</li>
    <li>Engage the architect the franchisor requires or approves to adapt the prototype to your site.</li>
    <li>Identify the brand-critical elements — feature walls, bulkheads, signage, finishes — that can’t change, and the items where substitutions are allowed.</li>
    <li>Ask your contractor to review the site-adapted set before it is finalized. Catching a conflict on paper is far cheaper than in the field.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="contractor">Choosing a contractor</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Look for commercial interior experience, and ideally prior work for franchise brands.</li>
    <li>Ask who performs the carpentry. Branded features like curved bulkheads and soffits are carpentry-heavy — see the <a href="/projects/aqua-tots-swim-schools/">Aqua-Tots soffits</a> or the <a href="/projects/menchies-frozen-yogurt/">Menchie’s yogurt bar wall</a>.</li>
    <li>Compare bids scope-for-scope against the franchisor’s drawings, including owner-furnished and franchisor-supplied items.</li>
    <li>Confirm the contractor can meet the landlord’s and franchisor’s insurance requirements.</li>
    <li>Agree on a schedule built backward from opening day, with franchisor walk-throughs and inspections on it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="permits">Approvals and permits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Franchisor approval of the site-adapted drawings.</li>
    <li>Landlord approval of drawings, contractor and insurance.</li>
    <li>Building permit from the local building department, plus mechanical, electrical and plumbing permits.</li>
    <li>Health department plan review for food concepts; fire-protection review where sprinklers or alarms change.</li>
    <li>Sign permits — often a separate application, and sometimes subject to landlord sign criteria too.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="construction">During construction</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Order long-lead items immediately: kitchen and specialty equipment, storefront glazing, signage, rooftop units.</li>
    <li>Track franchisor-supplied fixtures and décor so they arrive when the space is ready for them, not before.</li>
    <li>Hold a short weekly update with the contractor covering schedule, open questions and decisions you owe.</li>
    <li>Avoid design changes after framing starts; when they are unavoidable, get the cost and schedule impact in writing first.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="opening">The last weeks before opening</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Schedule final inspections and the health inspection (for food concepts) with buffer before training starts.</li>
    <li>Walk the space with your contractor and the franchisor’s representative to build one combined punch list.</li>
    <li>Collect closeout documents: warranties, equipment manuals and as-built information.</li>
    <li>Plan equipment start-up and staff training after the certificate of occupancy, not before.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="next-unit">Building your next unit</h2>
  <p>
    Multi-unit franchisees gain the most from consistency. Keeping the same contractor means the lessons from
    location one — what the prototype gets wrong, which details take longest, which vendors deliver late — carry straight
    into location two. That is why franchise clients like Aqua-Tots, NKD Pizza and Menchie’s have returned to Meridian
    for additional locations.
  </p>
